# Python Installation and Development Environment

Install Python from the official Python website.

[Python Downloads](https://www.python.org/downloads/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### IDE

* Visual Studio Code

### Verify Python Installation

```bash
python --version
```

or:

```bash
python3 --version
```

---

# Python Scripts

Python scripts are `.py` files containing Python code.

Example:

```python
print("Hello World")
```

Run the script:

```bash
python sample.py
```

---

# Python Web Applications

A web application allows users to interact with an application through a web browser.

Python provides several web application frameworks:

```text
Python
   |
   +-- Flask
   |
   +-- Django
   |
   +-- Streamlit
```

---

# Flask Web Application Framework

**Flask** is a lightweight Python web application framework.

It can be used to create web applications and REST APIs.

## Create a Flask Project

Create a project folder:

```text
My-WebApp-Project/
│
├── app.py
│
└── templates/
    └── sample.html
```

The `templates` directory is used to store HTML templates.

---

# Install Flask

Open a terminal inside the project directory:

```bash
pip install flask
```

Verify Flask installation:

```bash
pip show flask
```

---

# Create `app.py`

```python
from flask import Flask, render_template

app = Flask(__name__)


@app.route("/")
def sample():
    return render_template("sample.html")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True)
```

### Explanation

```python
from flask import Flask, render_template
```

Imports Flask and the `render_template` function.

```python
app = Flask(__name__)
```

Creates the Flask application.

```python
@app.route("/")
```

Defines the URL route for the application's home page.

```python
def sample():
```

Defines the Python function that handles the request.

```python
return render_template("sample.html")
```

Loads and displays `sample.html` from the `templates` directory.

```python
app.run(debug=True)
```

Starts the Flask development server with debug mode enabled.

---

# Create `templates/sample.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Python Flask Web Application</title>

    <link
        rel="stylesheet"
        href="https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/3.3.6/css/bootstrap.min.css">
</head>

<body>

<div class="jumbotron" style="background-color: white">

    <img
        src="https://octodex.github.com/images/spidertocat.png"
        alt="Spidertocat"
        class="img-responsive center-block"
        style="width: 250px">

    <h2 class="text-center">Hello Team</h2>

    <p class="text-center">
        Python Flask Web App Demo.
    </p>

</div>

</body>
</html>
```

---

# Run the Flask Application

From the project directory:

```bash
python -m flask run
```

The Flask development server runs on:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:5000
```

### Default Flask Port

```text
5000
```

Therefore:

```text
Application
    |
    v
Flask
    |
    v
127.0.0.1:5000
    |
    v
Web Browser
```

---

# Flask Project Flow

```text
Create Project
      |
      v
Create app.py
      |
      v
Install Flask
      |
      v
Create templates directory
      |
      v
Create HTML template
      |
      v
Define Flask Route
      |
      v
Run Flask Application
      |
      v
http://127.0.0.1:5000
```

# Key Points

* Python is used for application development, scripting, automation, DevOps, and MLOps.
* Flask is a lightweight Python web framework.
* Django is a full-featured Python web framework.
* Streamlit is useful for quickly building data, AI/ML, and API-based web applications.
* Flask uses **routes** to map URLs to Python functions.
* HTML templates are commonly stored inside the `templates` directory.
* `render_template()` is used to render an HTML template.
* Flask's default development server uses port **5000**.
